{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ORPHAN CAUGHT AND KILLED — a lesson the repo's own detector could not have given me.\nI stopped the parity subagent (its work was already committed and independently verified by me, and\nit had entered a loop re-arming waits on a pytest run I had earlier killed). Stopping the AGENT\norphaned the pytest run it had spawned: PID 18811 reparented to PPID 1, still burning CPU and still\ncontending for this worktree against my authoritative run.\nscripts/check_orphaned_load.py did NOT flag it, correctly by its own rules — it reports processes\nwhose accumulated CPU exceeds one hour, and this one was ~16 minutes in. So the detector is a\nbackstop for long-running leaks, not a substitute for reaping what you spawn.\nGENERALISABLE: killing a subagent does NOT reap that subagent's background children. Anything it\nstarted with run_in_background survives it and reparents to PID 1. Check by command line\n(pgrep -fl) after stopping an agent, not by trusting the CPU-threshold detector.\nKilled with -9; load back to 7.8 with only the properly-parented run remaining.\n\nThat subagent also self-reported two instrumentation errors worth keeping: it first lost a result to\noutput buffered behind 'tail' when its run was killed (exit 144), then armed a wait using 'timeout',\nwhich does NOT exist on this macOS host (GNU coreutils; I hit the same thing earlier in this run) so\nit exited instantly instead of waiting. Neither was a test failure. It reported both plainly rather\nthan papering over them, which is why they are recorded here."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788325478885104001,"uuid":"3ce7a78e-17b9-468a-93b1-4665f2dc4168"}